   "source": [
    "# Quiz: Making Meaningful Visualizations\n",
    "\n",
    "> **On Alps:** These are the questions of this lesson's pre-lecture and post-lecture quizzes, from the course's quiz app, with the answers the quiz app accepts."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Pre-lecture quiz"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "metadata": {
    "format": "application/vnd.pretext+xml"
   },
   "source": [
    "<exercise label=\"dsb-quiz-13-pre-1\">\n",
    "  <statement>\n",
    "    <p>It's relatively easy to deceive users by editing charts</p>\n",
    "  </statement>\n",
    "  <choices randomize=\"no\">\n",
    "    <choice correct=\"yes\">\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>True</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Correct. The eye is easily fooled, for example by a reversed axis or rearranged dates.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>False</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. Charts can easily fool the eye, for example by reversing an axis or rearranging dates.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "  </choices>\n",
    "</exercise>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "metadata": {
    "format": "application/vnd.pretext+xml"
   },
   "source": [
    "<exercise label=\"dsb-quiz-13-pre-2\">\n",
    "  <statement>\n",
    "    <p>Selecting the kind of chart to build depends on the type of data you have and the story you are telling about it</p>\n",
    "  </statement>\n",
    "  <choices randomize=\"no\">\n",
    "    <choice correct=\"yes\">\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>True</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Correct. You pick the chart that fits your data and the question you are asking.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>False</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. The right chart depends on your data and on the question you are asking.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "  </choices>\n",
    "</exercise>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "metadata": {
    "format": "application/vnd.pretext+xml"
   },
   "source": [
    "<exercise label=\"dsb-quiz-13-pre-3\">\n",
    "  <statement>\n",
    "    <p>To create a deceptive chart, some creators manipulate:</p>\n",
    "  </statement>\n",
    "  <choices randomize=\"no\">\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>the chart colors</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. Color can be used to deceive, but so can a flipped axis.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>the X and Y axes</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. A flipped axis can deceive, but so can color.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice correct=\"yes\">\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>either of the above</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Correct. Deceptive charts can use color, a flipped axis, or both.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "  </choices>\n",
    "</exercise>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Post-lecture quiz"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "metadata": {
    "format": "application/vnd.pretext+xml"
   },
   "source": [
    "<exercise label=\"dsb-quiz-13-post-1\">\n",
    "  <statement>\n",
    "    <p>Ensure that your charts are:</p>\n",
    "  </statement>\n",
    "  <choices randomize=\"no\">\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>accessible</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. Charts should be accessible, but they also need to be readable.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>readable</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. Charts should be readable, but also accessible, for example to color-blind users.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice correct=\"yes\">\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>both of the above</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Correct. Charts should be both accessible, including to color-blind users, and readable.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "  </choices>\n",
    "</exercise>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "metadata": {
    "format": "application/vnd.pretext+xml"
   },
   "source": [
    "<exercise label=\"dsb-quiz-13-post-2\">\n",
    "  <statement>\n",
    "    <p>D3 is an excellent charting library for use to create:</p>\n",
    "  </statement>\n",
    "  <choices randomize=\"no\">\n",
    "    <choice correct=\"yes\">\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>animated visualizations</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Correct. Some of the best animated visualizations, like \"film flowers,\" were made with D3.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>infographics</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. The D3 examples here are animated visualizations, like \"film flowers\" and an animated social network.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>deep learning</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. D3 is a visualization library, used here to build animated charts.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "  </choices>\n",
    "</exercise>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "metadata": {
    "format": "application/vnd.pretext+xml"
   },
   "source": [
    "<exercise label=\"dsb-quiz-13-post-3\">\n",
    "  <statement>\n",
    "    <p>Ensure the readability of your chart by:</p>\n",
    "  </statement>\n",
    "  <choices randomize=\"no\">\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>adding tags</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. For readability, label your axes and angle long labels so they are easy to read.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>editing color</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. Color carries meaning and affects accessibility. For readability, label axes and angle long labels.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice correct=\"yes\">\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>aligning labels properly</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Correct. Clear labels help readability, such as angling long text on the X axis.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "  </choices>\n",
    "</exercise>"
   ]
  }
 ],
